1
00:00:09,441 --> 00:00:15,115
Same airstrip, San Pedro de la
Cueva, Sonora. One of these is real.

2
00:00:16,432 --> 00:00:23,063
I'm Wayne… our free GeezerGeek Pilot
app puts Mexico's remote airstrips,

3
00:00:23,063 --> 00:00:27,721
Baja and mainland, into
ForeFlight and X-Plane.

4
00:00:29,327 --> 00:00:30,285
On this trip,

5
00:00:30,285 --> 00:00:36,717
all ForeFlight had was a waypoint I'd
typed in by latitude and longitude.

6
00:00:36,717 --> 00:00:39,857
No runway, no ramp, no pattern.

7
00:00:41,138 --> 00:00:45,554
Airstrips like this get
our FlyingDocs volunteers

8
00:00:45,554 --> 00:00:49,332
to the free medical and
dental clinics we provide.

9
00:00:49,332 --> 00:00:52,987
Mexico's FAA counterpart, AFAC,

10
00:00:52,987 --> 00:00:58,888
publishes the official spreadsheet
of more than 1,500 airstrips,

11
00:00:58,888 --> 00:01:02,436
about 500 of them currently permitted.

12
00:01:03,333 --> 00:01:09,860
Find the airstrip by name or
code, and you get AFAC's record:

13
00:01:09,860 --> 00:01:16,254
4,265 feet of asphalt, runway 16/34,
day only. English by default,

14
00:01:16,254 --> 00:01:19,206
Spanish with a tap.

15
00:01:20,119 --> 00:01:24,530
AFAC's coordinates are sometimes
off. At Laguna San Ignacio,

16
00:01:24,530 --> 00:01:26,159
the whale-watching spot,

17
00:01:26,159 --> 00:01:30,856
just drag the runway end to
where the runway surface starts.

18
00:01:31,704 --> 00:01:35,368
The preview shows the runway
by surface, the ramp,

19
00:01:35,368 --> 00:01:39,355
and a dashed traffic pattern,
whose width is adjustable.

20
00:01:47,602 --> 00:01:53,791
On your iPad or iPhone, tap Open in
ForeFlight, install the pack under More,

21
00:01:53,791 --> 00:01:58,987
Custom Content, and turn on the
two new layers: the drawing,

22
00:01:58,987 --> 00:02:02,564
and a named point mid-runway. There it is.

23
00:02:03,899 --> 00:02:09,030
Tap on an airport waypoint, or type its
name, to add it to your flight plan.

24
00:02:10,422 --> 00:02:12,487
Beats hand-typed coordinates.

25
00:02:19,648 --> 00:02:24,443
Now let's detail an airstrip for
X-Plane. Here's Scorpion Bay,

26
00:02:24,443 --> 00:02:26,593
on Baja's Pacific side.

27
00:02:27,488 --> 00:02:33,877
No mapped ramp here, so the app adds
one. Drag it and size it to the imagery.

28
00:02:34,775 --> 00:02:40,045
Tie-downs are where you'll start, so put
them on the ramp, not in the cactus.

29
00:02:40,045 --> 00:02:43,475
Then a windsock, a hangar, a fuel tank.

30
00:02:44,470 --> 00:02:48,348
Your layout's saved, so you can
come back and keep fiddling.

31
00:02:49,242 --> 00:02:54,077
Download the zip, unzip it
into Custom Scenery, done.

32
00:02:54,953 --> 00:02:58,018
If X-Plane already has an airport there,

33
00:02:58,018 --> 00:03:01,293
Import from Gateway lets you build on it.

34
00:03:13,499 --> 00:03:16,325
And parked right where I put the tie-down.

35
00:03:17,591 --> 00:03:22,941
One caution: this app is for planning
and preparation for your flight,

36
00:03:22,941 --> 00:03:28,702
in real life or on the sim. It doesn't
tell you whether an airstrip is open,

37
00:03:28,702 --> 00:03:33,208
in good shape, or needs
permission. Check before you go.

38
00:03:35,179 --> 00:03:39,455
Thanks again to Roberto
Carlos and to AFAC officials,

39
00:03:39,455 --> 00:03:43,549
both in place at staffed
airports and at headquarters.

40
00:03:43,549 --> 00:03:49,201
The app's free on my GeezerGeek
Pilot Control Panel; link below.

41
00:03:49,201 --> 00:03:53,788
Please give me some feedback on
which airstrip you build first.

